Title: Casebook chapter about the opinion: Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp.

# Foundations of Trademark Registration and Likelihood of Confusion

This chapter explains how the Lanham Act’s prohibition against confusingly similar marks (Section 2(d)) is applied at the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) and reviewed by the Federal Circuit. It uses Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., 2025 WL ___ (Fed. Cir. Sept. 25, 2025), as the doctrinal touchstone for several recurring issues: how to define the goods or services, the proper use of third‑party evidence, the interdependence of the DuPont factors, and the standards of review on appeal. The goal is to give you a practical, principled method for analyzing likelihood of confusion disputes and for preserving issues for appeal.

## Section 2(d): Purpose and the Policy Against Consumer Confusion

Section 2(d) of the Lanham Act, 15 U.S.C. § 1052(d), forbids registration of a mark when “confusion is likely because of concurrent use of the marks of an applicant and a prior user on their respective goods.” The statutory policy is straightforward: the federal registration system should not sanction marks that are likely to cause consumers to mistake the source of goods or services. Likelihood of confusion litigation or opposition is thus not a vehicle to vindicate abstract rights of stylistic preference; it is a consumer‑protection inquiry rooted in marketplace perception.

Two corollaries flow from that purpose. First, the inquiry is pragmatic and fact‑intensive: courts and the Board ask whether consumers are likely to be misled, not whether lawyers or academics find two marks elegant or ugly. Second, the analysis is source‑oriented. The operative question is whether the marks, as used on the specific goods or services at issue, are likely to make consumers believe that the products or services come from the same source (or are affiliated).

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. illustrates these principles. CC Serve opposed registration of Apex’s ASPIRE BANK marks for banking and financing services on the ground they would cause confusion with CC Serve’s existing ASPIRE registration for credit card services. The Board and the Federal Circuit framed the dispute as whether consumers would likely mistake the two ASPIRE‑formative marks for the same source of financial services. The Court emphasized that this is a holistic, consumer‑facing inquiry informed by the DuPont factors discussed below.

## TTAB Practice: Ex parte Examination, Letters of Protest, Oppositions, and the Record on Appeal

The modern path to litigation at the TTAB typically begins at the PTO examining attorney. After an application is filed, an examiner reviews it for statutory compliance and conflicts with registered marks. Opponents may try to influence the examiner by filing a “letter of protest” or similar submissions, but such filings do not substitute for a formal opposition. If the examiner approves an application for publication, third parties then have the statutory window to oppose registration.

If a formal opposition is filed, the TTAB conducts an adversarial proceeding that resembles a bench trial: parties take discovery, submit evidence (testimony and exhibits), and present arguments. The Board’s decision rests entirely on the administrative record compiled during that proceeding; documents that were not part of the record cannot be considered on appeal. Thus, if a party wants a piece of evidence considered by the Board, that evidence must be introduced into the TTAB record at the opposition proceeding.

In Apex, CC Serve filed a letter of protest during prosecution and later brought a formal opposition after the mark published. The Board treated the materials properly introduced during the opposition as the evidentiary record. On appeal to the Federal Circuit, the Court reviewed the Board’s written decision and the trial record, applying the familiar administrative review standards discussed below.

Practical point: if you are advising a client at the application stage, consider both ex parte submissions (letters of protest) and the more extensive—but more powerful—opposition procedure. Make sure any facts you will later rely on are developed and entered into the TTAB record during the opposition.

## The DuPont Multi‑Factor Test: Origin, Content, Weighting, and Interdependence

Since Application of E. I. DuPont DeNemours & Co., 476 F.2d 1357 (C.C.P.A. 1973), courts and the TTAB evaluate likelihood of confusion under a flexible, multi‑factor test. The original DuPont opinion articulated thirteen factors; later cases have distilled and reordered them, but the factors remain the analytical toolbox:

- Similarity of the marks in appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression (DuPont factor 1).
- Similarity or relatedness of the goods or services and the established, likely channels of trade (factor 2).
- The conditions under which and buyers to whom sales are made (care exercised by consumers) (factor 3).
- The fame of the prior mark (factor 4).
- The number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods (factor 6).
- Evidence of actual confusion (factor 7).
- The length and exclusivity of concurrent use (factor 10).
- Applicant’s intent in adopting the mark (factor 11).
- The quality of the applicant’s goods or services (factor 12).
- Any other established fact probative of the effect of use (catch‑all).

The DuPont factors are not a checklist that must be mechanically applied; the Board need only consider those factors that are “relevant and of record.” M2 Software, Inc. v. M2 Communications, Inc., 450 F.3d 1378, 1382 (Fed. Cir. 2006). But two methodological constraints are important.

First, the DuPont factors are interdependent. Findings under one factor can and often should inform findings under another. For example, if the Board finds that the parties’ services are highly similar (factor 2), that should inform the scope of third‑party marks that are relevant under factor 6 and the analysis of commercial impression under factor 1. Apex Bank demonstrates this interdependence: the Board found the services “highly similar” or partially legally identical under factor 2, yet then—erroneously, in the Federal Circuit’s view—narrowed the universe of third‑party marks under factor 6 to only those covering “credit card services,” excluding marks used in other banking and financial contexts that the Board had already deemed similar. The Federal Circuit vacated the Board’s factor‑6 analysis and remanded because the Board’s inconsistent scopes undercut the DuPont interdependence principle.

Second, DuPont is flexible about weight. No single factor is dispositive in every case. Courts have frequently said that the marks’ similarity (factor 1) and the goods’ relatedness (factor 2) are often central; actual confusion (factor 7), when it exists, is highly probative. But the particular factual mix—fame of the mark, crowdedness of the field, and consumer sophistication—determines weight in the individual dispute.

A structured way to apply DuPont on a record:
1. Define the goods or services precisely as they appear in the registrations or applications. If definitions are ambiguous, develop the record (e.g., through dictionary evidence or the Trademark ID Manual).
2. Identify the relevant consumer group and channels of trade.
3. Analyze marks’ similarity (appearance, sound, meaning, commercial impression).
4. Assess relatedness of goods/services and overlap of trade channels.
5. Evaluate third‑party evidence and strength of the mark (conceptual and commercial strength).
6. Consider other factors (actual confusion, intent, consumer care) and weigh everything holistically to answer whether confusion is likely.

## Evidence Commonly Used Before the TTAB

The Board and courts rely on several common categories of evidence. Understanding the evidentiary value and limitations of each is critical.

- Third‑party registrations. Registrations show that the PTO has allowed other parties to register similar marks for particular goods/services. Registrations are persuasive evidence that goods or services can emanate from a single source under a single mark, and that the mark in question is not unique in the field. But registrations do not by themselves prove actual marketplace use or consumer recognition; they merely reflect registration outcomes. Apex’s record included multiple third‑party registrations using ASPIRE or Aspire‑formative marks. The Board counted such registrations but then—contrary to the Court’s view—narrowed consideration improperly when assessing weakness.

- Third‑party uses (web pages, catalogs, screenshots, business records). Actual use evidence (sales, advertising, websites) is stronger than registrations because it shows marketplace exposure and helps measure commercial strength. The Board may treat such evidence as demonstrative of how consumers experience marks.

- Evidence of actual confusion (witness testimony, declarations, misdirected communications). Actual confusion is highly probative but rare. The Board looks for contemporaneous, thoughtful evidence (dates and specifics), not speculative inferences.

- Dictionary definitions and industry trade publications. These can help define terms in identifications of goods and services, or show the ordinary meaning of words used in marks. In Apex, because the PTO’s ID Manual had removed an entry for “credit card services,” the Board relied on definitional materials to characterize CC Serve’s services comprehensively.

- Internet evidence (screenshots, press releases). These are admissible if properly authenticated, and they help show channels of trade, extent of advertising, and third‑party uses. Because the TTAB record is the basis for appeal, parties must authenticate and enter online evidence in the proceeding.

- The USPTO Trademark ID Manual and TESS. The ID Manual is a tool for identifying pre‑approved descriptions of goods and services; it sometimes defines coverage that an examiner will accept without amendment. But the ID Manual is not dispositive of whether services are identical or related for DuPont purposes; it is one piece of guidance, and the Board will interpret identifications in the context of the record.

Practical evidence strategy: favor actual use evidence to show market presence and consumer exposure, and calibrate the geographic and trade channel overlap. If you rely on registrations, include the registration certificates, dates, and relevant descriptions so the Board can evaluate their relevance.

## Conceptual Strength vs. Commercial Strength; Inherent Distinctiveness and Scope of Protection

A mark’s protectable scope depends on both conceptual strength (inherent distinctiveness) and commercial strength (market recognition and fame).

- Conceptual strength is categorical. Marks fall along the classic spectrum: generic (no protection), descriptive (protection only upon acquired distinctiveness), suggestive (inherently distinctive), arbitrary and fanciful (strongest inherent distinctiveness). A mark’s inherent distinctiveness affects how much protection it receives: arbitrary/fanciful marks enjoy the broadest scope.

- Commercial strength (market strength) measures marketplace renown: consumer recognition, market share, advertising expenditures, and length of exclusive use. Famous marks (e.g., Vuitton, Coke) enjoy broader protection even against dissimilar marks.

The sixth DuPont factor—“the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods”—is the principal vehicle for analyzing weakness or crowdedness. If a field is crowded with similar marks, courts treat a particular mark as relatively weak and entitled to a narrower scope of protection: consumers accustomed to many similar marks will be better able to distinguish them. Apex demonstrates that when the Board finds the parties’ goods/services to be highly similar, it must consider third‑party marks across the whole range of similar services, not an artificially narrowed class. In Apex the Board initially found the services highly similar (suggesting a broad class of related financial services), but then limited factor‑6 analysis to only registrations that explicitly stated “credit card services.” The Federal Circuit vacated that approach because the DuPont interdependence principle requires consistent scope.

Another important point concerns standard character marks (also called word marks) versus stylized word and design marks. A standard character mark registration claims rights in the word irrespective of font, style, or design. That confers a broader claim to the word element universally; design elements of a later applicant may be less probative to avoid confusion if the prior registrant has a strong standard character mark. In Apex, CC Serve owned a standard-character ASPIRE registration, which carries the presumption of exclusive use of the word ASPIRE in the registered services and gives the Board a starting presumption about the mark’s strength and scope (subject to rebuttal by third‑party evidence).

## Burdens of Proof, Presumptions, and Priority

In TTAB oppositions, the party asserting likelihood of confusion (the opposer) bears the burden of proving that confusion is likely by a preponderance of the evidence. A prior federal registration confers a bundle of presumptions in favor of the registrant: prima facie validity, ownership, and a right to exclude others within the registered goods/services. These presumptions shift the practical battleground: a challenger who attempts to register a mark must overcome the presumption of registrability afforded to the prior registrant’s mark.

Priority of use is also central in contested proceedings. A registrant’s filing and registration dates can establish constructive nationwide priority as of the date of filing or registration, and the registrant’s actual date of first use may establish earlier common‑law rights. In Apex, CC Serve’s registration had an effective priority date of October 17, 1996, a clear advantage to CC Serve on the priority question.

That said, a prior registration does not automatically doom an applicant. The Board still asks whether confusion is likely, applying the DuPont factors. The registrant’s presumptions are probative and powerful, but they do not replace the multi‑factor inquiry.

## Standards of Review on Appeal to the Federal Circuit

Two basic standards govern Federal Circuit review of TTAB decisions:

- Legal conclusions by the Board are reviewed de novo. The ultimate determination of likelihood of confusion is a legal conclusion that rests on subsidiary factual findings; therefore, the Court reviews legal error without deference.

- Factual findings are reviewed for substantial evidence. Substantial evidence means “such relevant evidence as a reasonable mind might accept as adequate to support a conclusion.” Consol. Edison Co. v. NLRB, 305 U.S. 197, 229 (1938). The Federal Circuit will uphold Board factual findings if supported by substantial evidence in the record, even if the court might have reached a different conclusion.

In practice, the Court often asks whether the Board applied the correct legal standards (e.g., proper definition of the relevant product universe) and then examines whether the Board’s fact findings are supported by substantial evidence. Apex followed this pattern. The Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s finding on DuPont factor 2 (relatedness of services) because substantial evidence supported it. But the Court held the Board made a legal error by applying an inconsistent scope when evaluating factor 6, i.e., using a narrow universe of third‑party marks despite having found the parties’ services to be highly similar. The Court therefore vacated the factor‑6 and factor‑1 analyses and remanded for reconsideration.

Jurisdiction over appeals from TTAB final decisions lies in the Federal Circuit under 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B). The appellate record is the TTAB record; the Federal Circuit does not take new evidence.

Practical appellate point: when presenting to the TTAB, make clear, well‑documented factual findings with record citations. If you want the Federal Circuit to review a legal error, preserve the issue in the Board and frame it as a legal question.

## Practical Guidance for Applying the Doctrine (Checklists and Common Pitfalls)

1. Define the goods and services precisely. The starting point is the identifications in the registrations or application. If a term is ambiguous or absent from the ID Manual, introduce dictionary definitions, industry materials, or other explanatory evidence during the TTAB proceeding.

2. Identify the relevant consumer. The relevant public can vary with the services at issue. Business buyers who exercise high care may be less likely to be confused; mass‑market consumers may be more susceptible. But don’t let an artificial narrowing of the consumer class defeat consideration of otherwise relevant third‑party evidence. Apex instructs that the Board must keep the same scope when moving among DuPont factors.

3. Present third‑party evidence carefully. Prefer evidence of actual use (web pages, sales) over mere registrations. If relying on registrations, explain their scope and why they are probative for the relevant range of services. Be sure to authenticate internet evidence and to enter it into the record.

4. Distinguish conceptual and commercial strength. If a mark is descriptively weak but enjoys strong market recognition, the Board must account for both. Conversely, a suggestive mark with many third‑party uses may be commercially weak even if conceptually strong.

5. Address likely channels of trade and classes of consumers. Show overlapping channels (same websites, same bundling of services, partnerships) to support relatedness under factor 2.

6. Preserve arguments about DuPont interdependence. If you rely on a broad relatedness finding under factor 2, explain why the same breadth must apply when assessing third‑party weakness under factor 6 and commercial impression under factor 1. Apex shows the Court will insist on internal consistency.

7. Authentication and contemporaneousness matter. For actual confusion, contemporaneous misdirected payments, consumer statements, or internal communications are far more persuasive than after‑the‑fact assertions.

8. When defending a registration, exploit presumptions attached to the prior registration (priority, validity, exclusive rights) but prepare to rebut third‑party evidence and to show lack of overlap in channels or consumer class where appropriate.

## Concluding Lessons from Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp.

Apex Bank exemplifies recurring TTAB and appellate themes:

- Likelihood of confusion is a legal determination grounded in factual DuPont findings; Federal Circuit review is de novo for legal errors and substantial‑evidence for facts.

- The Board must apply DuPont factors flexibly but consistently. If the Board finds goods/services highly similar under factor 2, it must not then narrow the third‑party mark universe under factor 6 to exclude categories of similar services it previously deemed relevant.

- Third‑party evidence is critical to assessing the relative strength of a mark, but the scope of that evidence must match the scope of the goods/services at issue.

- Standard character registrations (word marks) and early priority dates give the prior registrant powerful presumptions, yet those presumptions are part of the overall DuPont mosaic rather than an automatic victory.

For practical advocacy before the TTAB or on appeal, build a record that (1) defines the class of goods/services and the relevant consumers, (2) authenticates use and registration evidence, (3) ties third‑party evidence to the defined scope consistently across DuPont factors, and (4) anticipates the Federal Circuit’s two‑tier review of legal conclusions and factual findings. Apex Bank demonstrates the cost of inconsistency: it can force a remand even where some findings (here, the relatedness of services) are solid under substantial evidence review.

# Relatedness of Services in Financial Brands: The Second DuPont Factor

The second DuPont factor—whether the goods or services are related—often decides a trademark dispute before the other factors meaningfully bite. In financial-brand cases, this factor frequently tilts in favor of likelihood of confusion because the industry contains many overlapping activities (issuing credit, servicing accounts, branding and co-branding, program management, and full-service banking). Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., a 2025 Federal Circuit decision, provides a clear illustration of how identifications, dictionary evidence, and third‑party registrations are used to define the scope of services and to establish that financial services are “related,” and in some respects “legally identical.” This chapter explains the applicable rules and tests, how they were applied in Apex, and practical rules for practitioners drafting and litigating identifications in the financial-services context.

## The controlling principle: compare the identifications, not commerce

A basic and recurring principle in trademark oppositions and cancellations is that the comparison of goods or services is governed by the identifications in the registration and the application—not by the actual products or services in the marketplace. Courts and the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) evaluate relatedness based on what the parties have claimed they will offer (or have registered), not on how they currently operate in the real world. This rule flows from the premise that a registration confers rights to the mark for the goods or services as they are identified.

Apex underscores that principle. CC Serve’s registration identified “credit card services” and Apex’s applications identified “banking and financing services.” The Board and ultimately the Federal Circuit started from those identifications; they did not restrict the analysis to the particular contractual arrangements in which CC Serve participated (where CC Serve acted as a program manager and partnered with banks to issue co‑branded cards). The Federal Circuit affirmed that the Board appropriately compared the scope of the parties’ identifications rather than their present business models, and that substantial evidence supported the Board’s finding of high similarity between the services. The lesson: when examining relatedness, the parties are judged by the labels they chose for their filings unless the identifications themselves require construction.

## Construe identifications when ID Manual entries change: the role of plain meaning and dictionaries

When a USPTO Identifications Manual entry (now commonly called the ID Manual) changes or a short, ambiguous identification appears, the Board and courts must construe the identification to determine what the words encompass. Apex presented exactly that situation: the entry for “credit card services” had been deleted from the ID Manual after CC Serve’s registration issued, so the Board needed to define the term’s ordinary meaning.

The Board used dictionary definitions and other authoritative sources to determine the plain, ordinary meaning of “credit card services,” “banking,” and “finance.” It concluded that those terms overlap because dictionaries and commonly accepted definitions show that banking and finance often include extending credit or providing funds—activities that may be accomplished through credit cards. The Federal Circuit accepted that approach: where an identification is unclear or the ID Manual no longer provides direct guidance, the Board may turn to dictionaries and other extrinsic evidence to determine the ordinary meaning of the terms used in the parties’ identifications.

Practical point: applicants should avoid vague, umbrella terms when a precise scope is intended; conversely, opponents should be prepared to submit dictionary and industry evidence to establish ordinary meanings when an identification must be construed.

## “Legally identical, in part”: what that phrase means and why it matters

Apex and CC Serve’s conflict resulted in the Board finding the services to be “legally identical, in part.” That formulation reflects a common scenario: the identifications are not fully identical word-for-word, but some of the identified services overlap such that a portion of the services covered by one party is the same as a portion covered by the other. When an overlap exists, courts treat that overlapping portion as legally identical.

Why does partial legal identity matter? Because when services are legally identical (even only in part), there is often a presumption that relatedness has been demonstrated. The practical consequence is that the opponent need not produce additional marketplace evidence to show relatedness; the overlap in the written identifications suffices to support a finding that the services are related for likelihood-of-confusion purposes. Once the overlap is established, the analysis moves on to the other DuPont factors (similarity of marks, channels of trade, commercial impression, strength of the mark, etc.), but the second factor itself will usually weigh in favor of confusion.

In Apex the Board concluded—and the Federal Circuit found supported by substantial evidence—that because “banking,” “bank,” and “finance” can encompass issuing credit cards and related account‑management services, the parties’ services overlapped in at least some respects. That overlap was sufficient to warrant calling them “highly similar” and “legally identical, in part.”

## Relatedness in the financial sector: program managers, co‑brand issuers, and banks

Financial services present particular challenges in relatedness analysis because the functions in the industry are frequently split among different actors and the same consumer often encounters different actors in a single product experience. Several configurations commonly appear:

- Traditional bank issuer: a bank issues a credit card and holds the account.
- Program manager / servicer: a nonbank firm (a card servicer or processor) provides program management, processing, and servicing for a card program while a bank technically issues the card.
- Co‑branding arrangements: multiple brands appear on the card (bank brand, retail brand, network mark).
- Full-service banking: a bank offers checking, savings, loans, mortgages, and sometimes credit cards.

In Coach Services v. Triumph Learning, the Federal Circuit emphasized that identical or closely related descriptions of services are sufficient to show relatedness even when services are not identical in business practice. Apex follows that logic. The fact that CC Serve’s business model involved partnering with banks to issue ASPIRE‑branded cards did not immunize its registration from a claim of relatedness with Apex’s “banking and financing services.” The relevant inquiry is whether consumers would plausibly perceive the services as emanating from a common source, not who technically issues payment instruments in contractual terms.

This is particularly important in the era of outsourcing and co-branded programs: a bank customer encountering an ASPIRE card and an ASPIRE BANK online banking portal might well perceive a common commercial source, even if different legal entities perform issuance vs. servicing. Hence the Board’s and the Federal Circuit’s focus on the ordinary meaning of the service terms that consumers encounter and whether those ordinary meanings overlap.

## Third‑party registrations: admissible but limited evidence that services commonly emanate from a single source

Third‑party registrations and examples of use are a regular feature of second-DuPont-factor proof. Such evidence is relevant to show that in the marketplace it is common for a single source to offer both categories of services, or that certain services are offered under the same mark by multiple proprietors. In Apex the Board relied on third‑party registrations showing marks that covered both credit‑card services and banking/financing services to support relatedness.

Two cautions about third‑party registrations:

- Registrations are not conclusive proof of use: they show that the USPTO once allowed those identifications and that registrants claimed use (or intent to use) for those services; they do not in themselves prove marketplace use or consumer perception. Nevertheless, they tend to show that registration practice recognizes overlap between categories and that other parties have claimed both services under single marks.
- The number and nature of third‑party marks matter: a handful of registrations that combine two fields may be persuasive evidence that the services can emanate from one source; an extensive body of third‑party registrations and demonstrated marketplace use can support a finding that a mark is conceptually or commercially weak in that field and entitled to a narrower scope of protection.

Apex also highlights an important interplay with the sixth DuPont factor (the number and nature of similar marks in use). Because the Board found the services highly similar on the second factor, the Federal Circuit held that the Board must consider third‑party marks across the full scope of the services it has found similar—not artificially restrict the universe to only a narrow subcategory. If the services are legally similar across banking and credit card services, evidence of Aspire‑formative marks in broader banking and finance contexts is relevant to show whether the mark is weak or how consumers discriminate among marks.

## Consequences for the overall likelihood-of-confusion analysis

When the second factor favors the senior user because the services are identical or highly similar, other DuPont factors often follow naturally. A legally identical or highly related service means:

- Channels of trade and classes of purchasers are more likely to overlap; the Board can presume that the channels and purchasers identified in the application and registration coincide unless the identifications include limiting language.
- Consumers may reasonably believe a common source exists for differently labeled services (e.g., a card and a bank account bearing the same dominant term).
- The senior user need not produce abundant marketplace pairing evidence; overlapping identifications and supporting third‑party registrations can be enough.

However, the strength of the senior user’s mark, third‑party use of similar marks, and differences in commercial impression still play crucial roles. A finding that services are related does not compel a finding of likelihood of confusion if other DuPont factors significantly favor the junior user (for example, if the senior mark is shown weak through extensive third‑party use, or the marks are dissimilar in appearance and sound).

Apex demonstrates this balanced approach: the Court affirmed the Board’s finding of high similarity for the second DuPont factor, but it vacated and remanded analysis of the first and sixth factors so the Board could re-evaluate the number and nature of third‑party Aspire marks across the full scope of services it had found similar, and then reconsider the marks’ overall commercial impressions.

## Practical drafting and prosecution strategies

How parties draft identifications and handle prosecution can materially affect a later confusion analysis. The financial‑services context offers several tactical choices:

- Tailor identifications precisely. If you are an applicant seeking a registration for a single activity (e.g., “account servicing for merchant credit card programs”), avoid broad umbrella terms like “banking services” or “financial services” unless you intend to claim them. Narrower identifications limit overlap and reduce exposure to oppositions.
- Use limiting language. If your services are restricted to particular customers, channels, or activities, state those limits explicitly (e.g., “credit card account servicing for supermarket co‑branded credit card programs”). The TTAB will usually respect clear limitation language when comparing identifications.
- Consider disclaimers. If a word in a mark is descriptive and conflicts with a senior user’s formative term, a disclaimer may help obtain a registration, though disclaimers do not necessarily avoid confusion claims.
- Try to secure consent/coexistence agreements. A consent from a prior user (with clear geographic or channel limitations) can be persuasive to the PTO and the Board and may be recorded with the USPTO to prevent later arguments about the proper scope of coexistence.
- Be strategic about amendments. Amending an identification to narrow scope during prosecution can reduce conflict with existing registrations. But be mindful: amendments may not cure prior oppositions if the opposer persuades a tribunal that confusion still remains.
- Preserve and submit industry evidence when needed. If the identifications require construction, be ready to present reliable dictionary definitions, industry standards, trade publications, or regulatory definitions to establish ordinary meaning.
- When litigating, remember the presumption rule: courts and the Board compare identifications as written; if those identifications are broad and overlap, a party claiming distinctness bears the burden of showing that the ordinary consumer would not perceive a relationship between them.

## Applying these rules to Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp.

Apex filed intent‑to‑use applications for ASPIRE BANK word and design marks for “banking and financing services.” CC Serve relied on a 1998 registration for ASPIRE for “credit card services.” The Board concluded that the services were highly similar and in part legally identical because common definitions of “banking” and “finance” include extending credit via credit cards. The Board supplemented the definitional evidence with third‑party registrations covering both credit card and banking/finance services. The Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s conclusion under the second DuPont factor, finding substantial evidence supported the Board’s construction of the identifications and its determination that the services were highly similar.

At the same time, the Federal Circuit vacated the Board’s analysis of the sixth DuPont factor because, although the Board found the services highly similar, it limited its search for third‑party Aspire‑formative marks to the narrower credit‑card‑services category rather than to the broader field of banking and finance. The Circuit directed the Board on remand to consider third‑party marks across the full scope of services it had already found similar, and to reassess the marks’ overall commercial impression (first DuPont factor) in light of any new findings about the mark’s strength or weakness.

The takeaways from Apex are concrete: (1) identifications control; (2) when ID Manual guidance is no longer available, dictionaries and ordinary meaning can be used to construe service terms; (3) partial legal identity is sufficient to weigh the second DuPont factor strongly in favor of relatedness; and (4) once services are found related, third‑party registration evidence must be considered in the correct scope when evaluating mark strength and commercial impression.

## Checklist for students and practitioners

- Begin any perception of relatedness analysis with the parties’ identifications. Do not recast the analysis around actual business models unless the identifications themselves are ambiguous.
- If the ID Manual does not resolve the meaning of an identification, be prepared to introduce dictionary, industry, or regulatory materials to establish the ordinary meaning.
- If one identification encompasses an activity included in the other identification (even if only partially), call the overlap “legally identical, in part”; treat the second DuPont factor as strongly favoring relatedness absent persuasive contrary evidence.
- Use third‑party registrations and marketplace examples to show that services commonly emanate from the same source, but note their evidentiary limits (registrations ≠ confirmed use).
- When the second factor favors relatedness, ensure the sixth and first factors examine third‑party evidence and commercial impression at the same scope—otherwise the Board’s analysis may be reversed or remanded (as in Apex).
- Draft identifications narrowly and with limiting language where possible to avoid unnecessary overlap; consider consent agreements or coexistence arrangements when overlap is unavoidable.

Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a useful, contemporary statement of how courts apply the second DuPont factor in the financial‑services context. It reinforces the control of written identifications, permits the use of dictionary and industry evidence to construe ambiguous terms, recognizes that partial legal identity is sufficient to establish relatedness, and requires consistent scope when considering third‑party evidence and commercial impression. These rules matter especially in finance, where the same consumer experience often spans issuance, branding, servicing, and banking.

# Crowded Fields and Mark Strength: The Sixth DuPont Factor

One of the most consequential yet often misunderstood DuPont factors is the sixth: the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods or services. That factor is the primary vehicle by which an accused infringer or defendant can demonstrate that a plaintiff’s mark is relatively weak and therefore entitled to a narrower scope of protection. This chapter explains the legal role of the sixth factor, how courts evaluate third‑party evidence, the required link between the definition of “similar goods/services” used across DuPont analyses, and practical evidentiary considerations—illustrated and shoe‑horned to the Federal Circuit’s treatment of the Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. appeal.

## Purpose of the sixth DuPont factor: showing weakness through crowded fields

The Lanham Act’s likelihood‑of‑confusion analysis asks courts to weigh multiple factors to decide whether consumers are likely to confuse two marks. The sixth DuPont factor—“the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods”—asks whether the marketplace already contains other marks similar to the plaintiff’s such that the plaintiff’s mark is commercially weak. When many third parties use similar marks on similar goods or services, courts infer that consumers have learned to distinguish those similar marks, and therefore the plaintiff’s mark should receive a narrower scope of protection. Put differently: a crowded field diminishes exclusivity.

Cases make two basic points about the sixth factor. First, third‑party evidence is relevant to commercial strength, not dispositive: substantial third‑party use suggests weakness; little evidence suggests strength. Second, the inquiry evaluates similarity of marks used on similar goods or services—identity is not required. Palm Bay Imps., Inc. v. Veuve Clicquot Ponsardin Maison Fondee En 1772 and later decisions underscore that registrations and evidence of use can be probative of weakness, and Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin show how courts treat the quantity and quality of that evidence when deciding whether a mark is “crowded.”

Apex illustrates these principles. CC Serve’s ASPIRE registration was contested by Apex Bank, which submitted evidence of multiple third‑party uses of “Aspire” and “Aspire‑formative” marks in the financial services field. The Board recognized some third‑party uses but concluded they were not “considerable” and therefore did not meaningfully reduce CC Serve’s mark strength. The Federal Circuit did not reject the Board’s ultimate resolution on strength; rather, it identified a legal error in the scope of the third‑party universe the Board examined. That error—treating the sixth factor as if it required a narrower goods/services match than the Board had already accepted for the second DuPont factor—undermined the analysis and required remand.

## Qualitative and quantitative showings: what counts as “considerable” or “ubiquitous”

No fixed numeric threshold determines when third‑party evidence makes a mark “weak.” Past decisions refer to “voluminous,” “considerable,” or “ubiquitous” evidence as sufficient to show a crowded field, but the number attached to those adjectives varies with context.

- Juice Generation treated 26 third‑party marks as “a considerable number” in a fairly narrow field (juice/smoothie retail). Jack Wolfskin found that a dozen‑plus examples of paw‑print usage constituted voluminous evidence of a crowded field when coupled with registrations and use. By contrast, a handful of third‑party uses often will not defeat protection for an inherently distinctive mark.
- Courts assess both quantity and quality. A small number of third‑party marks may suffice if they are prominent, national in scope, longstanding, and clearly target the same consumers through the same channels. Conversely, many obscure or local uses may carry less weight.
- Context matters: the narrower and more specialized the goods or services, the fewer examples needed to demonstrate crowding. For broad, heterogeneous fields (e.g., “financial services” generally), more examples will be required to show that consumers have been exposed to similar marks on similar offerings.

Apex underscores that courts must evaluate third‑party evidence in the context of the relevant goods/services and public. The Board found nine Aspire‑formative marks in the credit‑card subfield and concluded that nine examples were not “considerable.” That factual judgment is not categorically wrong, but the Federal Circuit required that the Board consider additional Aspire uses in banking and financing fields once it had already found the parties’ services to be highly similar. Whether the additional evidence will push the tally into “considerable” territory is a fact question for the Board on remand.

## Registrations versus marketplace use: the evidentiary roles

Third‑party registrations and evidence of use play overlapping but distinct roles.

- Registrations: Third‑party registrations (or applications) can suggest that many others seek to use similar marks in the market. Registrations are admissible as evidence and can support a finding of crowding, but they are weaker than proof of actual marketplace presence because a registration alone does not show consumer exposure or commercial significance.
- Actual use: Evidence of marketplace use—screenshots of websites, marketing materials, press releases announcing national rollouts, transaction records, or other proof showing consumers have actually encountered the mark—carries greater weight. Use demonstrates that consumers have been (or are being) exposed to those marks and thus are more likely to have learned to distinguish them.
- Together: A combination of many registrations and significant use examples is most persuasive. Where registrations are numerous but show only small, localized, or non‑overlapping uses, courts will discount their effect.

Apex is instructive: the Board considered some third‑party examples but excluded many Aspire registrations and uses in related banking and financing services. The Federal Circuit required reconsideration because the Board’s exclusion meant it did not fully assess the marketplace presence of Aspire‑formative marks across the services it had found relevant.

## Defining “similar goods/services” and the requirement of consistency across DuPont analyses

A recurring source of error is treating “similar” differently across DuPont factors. The courts require internal consistency: the definition of the relevant goods or services used to evaluate similarity under factor two should inform the sixth‑factor inquiry.

- The second DuPont factor asks whether the parties’ goods/services are related such that consumers might believe they come from the same source. That inquiry can produce a factual finding that the services are highly similar or even legally identical in part.
- Once the Board (or court) has defined the commercial scope of the services relevant to the likelihood‑of‑confusion dispute, it cannot then narrow the scope for the sixth factor without justification. If the parties’ services are found highly similar or overlapping, third‑party marks used on any services within that same defined scope are potentially probative of crowding.
- Olde Tyme Foods and Juice Generation reinforce that “similar” does not mean “identical,” and the Federal Circuit in Apex makes the point explicit: when the Board found the parties’ services to be highly similar (banking/finance and credit‑card services), it erred by confining its sixth‑factor review to credit‑card marks only.

Practical effect: the Board must first define the relevant goods and the relevant consumer/public, based on its factor‑two analysis and the record. Then, when counting and qualifying third‑party marks, it should use that same definition unless record evidence or legal reasons suggest a narrower or broader scope is appropriate.

## Avoiding errors in defining the relevant public: adjacent services matter

Closely related to the scope of services is the identity of the relevant consumer or “relevant public.” The audience whose perception matters for both similarity and strength is the class of consumers who encounter the goods/services. The Board erred in Apex by treating the relevant public as only consumers of credit‑card services after finding the parties’ services broadly overlapping with banking and financing services.

Guidance:

- If the parties’ services reach the same end users (e.g., retail banking customers, consumers who use credit products) or travel through the same channels (e.g., online banking portals, co‑branded credit cards, bank branch networks), that shared audience is the relevant public for the sixth factor.
- Even if one party functions as a service provider to institutions (e.g., back‑end processing for bank‑issued credit cards) and the other interacts directly with retail customers, third‑party marks targeting the shared end users or marketed in the same channels can be probative of crowding.
- In financial services, adjacency is frequent: credit cards, checking and savings accounts, payment processing, loan origination, and account servicing often coexist in the same consumer minds and in the same marketing channels (banks, fintech portals, co‑brand partnerships). Excluding adjacent subfields without record support risks undercounting relevant third‑party usage.

Apex teaches that the Board must define the relevant public consistently and include adjacent services that its own factor‑two findings show are materially similar.

## Evaluating evidentiary weight: specimens, web pages, and marketplace context for financial brands

When preparing or evaluating a sixth‑factor record, counsel should gather and present evidence calibrated to the features courts value:

- Consumer‑facing materials showing actual use: website screenshots (with timestamps), mobile app listings, advertisements, social media profiles, press releases describing consumer rollouts, and consumer complaint or review pages demonstrate exposure.
- Business documents that show breadth of use: filings (SEC, state banking authorities), annual reports, bank press releases announcing co‑branding partnerships (e.g., a bank issuing an Aspire‑branded card through a servicing company), and merchant acceptance announcements all show marketplace presence.
- Geographical and channel coverage: evidence that a mark is used nationally (or within the same local market) and through the same channels as the parties’ services strengthens the showing. Localized or pilot uses are weaker.
- Duration and prominence: long‑standing brands and high‑profile national campaigns carry more weight than short‑lived or minor uses.
- Third‑party registrations: include them, but pair registrations with use evidence where possible. Registrations alone can be helpful in demonstrating how many others have sought to use a term, but courts will look for commercial exposure to conclude that consumers have actually learned to distinguish marks.

For financial services, particular attention should be paid to partnerships and co‑branding arrangements (banks issuing cards under a brand developed by a merchant or servicer), because such arrangements show overlapping commercial channels and a likelihood that consumers associate brands across those channels.

## Application to Apex: what the Federal Circuit required and why

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a compact example of the nexus between the second and sixth DuPont factors:

- The Board found that Apex’s proposed marks for “banking and financing services” and CC Serve’s earlier registration for “credit card services” were highly similar and even partially legally identical in operation. That finding defined a broad universe of overlapping services and a relevant consumer public.
- When the Board then considered the sixth DuPont factor, it limited its review to third‑party marks used only for credit‑card services and discarded Aspire‑formative marks used in broader banking and financing contexts. The Federal Circuit held this inconsistent: once the Board established the high similarity of services, it could not narrow the field of third‑party marks for the sixth factor compared to the definition used for factor two.
- The Circuit vacated the Board’s sixth‑factor finding and remanded for reconsideration of third‑party evidence in light of the Board’s own finding about the parties’ services. Because the sixth‑factor result may affect the commercial impression analysis under the first DuPont factor, the Court also remanded factor one for reconsideration.

The lesson for practitioners and exam writers is clear: the Board must (1) make a principled, record‑based definition of the relevant goods/services and relevant consumer for factor two; (2) employ that same definition when gathering and weighing third‑party evidence under factor six (unless there is a reasoned basis to adjust); and (3) evaluate third‑party registrations in tandem with evidence of actual marketplace use to decide whether the field is sufficiently crowded to erode a mark’s strength.

## Practical advice for litigators and exam takers

- Build the record holistically. If you plan to argue crowdedness, collect registrations, but prioritize contemporaneous use evidence—web archives, screenshots, press releases, consumer notices, and partnership announcements. For financial services, show co‑branding, bank partner materials, and consumer‑facing product descriptions.
- Define the relevant public early and consistently. If you win on a broad similarity finding under factor two, be prepared to resist narrowing under factor six or explain why a narrower subfield is appropriate.
- Quantify thoughtfully but remember there are no bright‑line counts. Emphasize quality (national, consumer‑facing, long‑running uses) as much as quantity.
- Anticipate cross‑effects among DuPont factors. A successful sixth‑factor showing can shrink the plaintiff’s market exclusivity and influence analysis under the first (similarity of marks) and overall likelihood‑of‑confusion balance.

In short: the sixth DuPont factor is a contextual inquiry into market crowding. Courts look for convincing evidence that consumers have been repeatedly exposed to similar marks in the same commercial space. Apex illustrates the procedural and doctrinal rule that the scope of “similar goods/services” for the sixth factor must align with the Board’s earlier determinations about the scope of the parties’ services—an alignment required to ensure a coherent, fair evaluation of a mark’s commercial strength.

# Comparing the Marks: Commercial Impression and the First DuPont Factor

This chapter explains how tribunals compare marks “in their entireties” for appearance, sound, connotation, and overall commercial impression—the first DuPont factor—and how that comparison interacts with the mark’s strength under the sixth DuPont factor. Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is the contemporary vehicle: the Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s finding that Apex’s and CC Serve’s services are highly similar, but vacated and remanded the Board’s analyses of the sixth and first DuPont factors because the Board improperly limited the universe of third‑party marks it considered. The lesson is threefold. First, mark comparison is holistic and marketplace‑focused, not an academic side‑by‑side parsing of words. Second, the commercial strength or weakness of a mark—shown through third‑party use and registration evidence—alters how similar two marks must be before confusion is likely. Third, error in assessing the field of third‑party use often requires rethinking the marks’ commercial impressions on remand.

## The totality standard: sight, sound, meaning, and overall commercial impression

The first DuPont factor requires courts and the TTAB to compare the marks “in their entireties as to appearance, sound, connotation and commercial impression.” DuPont, 476 F.2d at 1361. That comparison is not a mechanical checklist or a paper comparison of strings of letters. Instead, tribunals must ask whether, taking the marks as consumers encounter them in the marketplace, the overall commercial impressions are likely to lead purchasers to believe the goods or services come from the same source.

Three practical guideposts follow:

- Focus on consumer perception. The inquiry asks what message a mark conveys to the relevant public, given the nature of the goods or services and the circumstances of their marketing. The test is whether confusion as to source is likely, not whether the marks could be distinguished on close inspection by an expert.

- Consider all dimensions together. Sight, sound, and meaning are evaluated in combination. Two marks that differ visually may still sound alike and have the same commercial implication, producing a confusing overall impression.

- Resist over‑literal parsing. Minor differences—added or disclaimed terms, punctuation, or small design elements—often do not prevent confusion when the dominant commercial impression remains the same.

I.AM.Symbolic, LLC v. Fitbug, Inc. (In re I.AM.Symbolic, LLC) reiterates that this factor is about the overall commercial impression. Even if differences can be articulated, the question is whether a reasonable consumer would be confused in the marketplace.

When applying this totality standard in new fact patterns, identify the mark as it will appear to consumers (standard character, word+design, stylization), imagine typical encounters with the mark (ads, product packaging, signage), and then ask whether those encounters would likely produce source confusion.

## Shared dominant terms and added matter: effect of descriptive additions

A common issue is marks that share a prominent term but differ by an added descriptive or generic word—e.g., ASPIRE v. ASPIRE BANK. The courts recognize that the presence of an added generic or descriptive term reduces but does not eliminate the risk of confusion. The deletion or addition of such matter will be weighed, but the dominant term often controls the commercial impression.

Key principles:

- The dominant element doctrine. When a multi‑word mark includes a distinctive word plus a descriptive word, the distinctive word will often dominate the commercial impression. For example, consumers are likely to focus on “Aspire” rather than the generic descriptor “Bank.”

- Descriptive additions are not dispositive. Even if a registrant disclaims a descriptive element or the applicant adds a descriptive term, that does not automatically prevent a finding of likelihood of confusion. Disclaimers affect the registerability and exclusivity of the disclaimed term but are not a shield against confusion findings.

- Context matters. If the descriptive term alters the product category or clarifies a different source (for example, by signaling a subsidiary line or a geographically restricted operation), it can reduce confusion. But where goods/services overlap substantially—as the Board found in Apex—the added matter’s ability to distinguish is limited.

Thus, in hypothetical disputes between marks like ASPIRE and ASPIRE BANK, courts will ask whether adding “BANK” meaningfully changes what consumers perceive about the source. If consumers encountering both marks in the same marketplace would still think a single source might be behind both, the added word will not save the junior mark.

## Standard character marks versus composite word‑and‑design marks; dominance of words

Marks may be claimed as standard character marks (pure words, no claimed design) or as composite word‑and‑design marks. The functional difference matters for the first DuPont factor because a standard character registration protects the word in any stylization, and a word element typically dominates the commercial impression of a composite mark.

Practical consequences:

- Words generally control. Where a composite mark consists of a prominent word plus minor design elements, the word will usually be the dominant source identifier. Courts will compare the word elements even when one party has a design mark.

- Standard character registrations are broad. A standard character mark gives the registrant rights in the word across fonts and stylizations, which tends to strengthen the word element’s claim to being the core commercial impression.

- Design elements can matter when they are distinctive and prominent. If a logo’s design is large, unique, and prominent, it may materially change the commercial impression despite identical words.

In Apex, CC Serve’s ASPIRE is a standard character registration, while Apex sought ASPIRE BANK in word and design formats. That factual posture emphasizes the weight of the shared word component: a standard character ASPIRE registration arguably gives CC Serve a strong claim to the word itself across contexts, which is why the Board’s and court’s attention to consumer impression is pivotal.

## How mark strength calibrates similarity analysis

Mark strength is a central modifier in likelihood‑of‑confusion analysis. Strength operates both conceptually (is the mark inherently distinctive or merely descriptive?) and commercially (is the mark widely used by others in the field?). The sixth DuPont factor—number and nature of similar marks in use—tests the field’s crowdedness and thereby the mark’s relative strength.

Two effects of strength are particularly important:

- Strong marks receive broader protection. An inherently distinctive mark that is not diluted by third‑party use is entitled to a wider radius of protection; even modest differences between marks may be insufficient to avoid confusion.

- Weak marks get narrower protection. Where a mark is conceptually weak (descriptive or suggestive) or is commercially weak because many competitors use similar marks, courts permit closer coexistence. Evidence of multiple third‑party uses of the same or similar term in related goods/services implies that consumers have been educated to distinguish among those marks, reducing the likelihood that a single similarity will lead to confusion.

The sixth factor is therefore not a sidebar to the first factor but an input that changes how the first factor should be weighed. A crowded field around “Aspire” in banking and finance makes it more plausible that ASPIRE and ASPIRE BANK can coexist; a sparsely used, distinctive ASPIRE strengthens CC Serve’s claim that ASPIRE BANK will cause confusion.

## I.AM.Symbolic and the role of commercial impression in the first factor

In I.AM.Symbolic the Federal Circuit emphasized that the first DuPont factor is about commercial impression. The opinion reminds practitioners that the comparison should be realistic and consumer‑oriented, and that the overall impression may be informed by other evidence (e.g., how the marks are actually used, advertising, and channels of trade). The case is useful doctrine for two points: first, it reiterates the holistic focus described above; second, it shows that a later re‑evaluation of the mark’s strength or marketplace context can and should lead to a new first‑factor analysis.

I.AM.Symbolic supports the appellate approach in Apex: because the Board’s flawed treatment of third‑party use affected the perceived strength of ASPIRE, the Board could not reliably conclude what the commercial impression of ASPIRE v. ASPIRE BANK would be in the marketplace. Where strength shifts, so may the overall commercial impression and, with it, the first factor.

## Application to Apex: why the Federal Circuit vacated the first‑factor analysis and plausible outcomes on remand

Apex supplies a concrete example of the interdependence between the sixth and first factors. The Board found the parties’ services highly similar—indeed partially legally identical—and then, in its sixth‑factor analysis, limited third‑party Aspire marks to those used strictly for “credit card services.” Because the Board had already found similarity across banking and credit card services, that narrowing discounted relevant third‑party uses in banking and finance. The Federal Circuit held that narrowing was error: if services are highly similar across banking and credit card offerings, the universe of third‑party marks to be considered should include those used in both fields. Because the sixth factor speaks directly to commercial strength, and because commercial strength influences the overall commercial impression (the first factor), the court vacated and remanded both factors together.

On remand the Board must consider all third‑party evidence of ASPIRE/Aspire‑formative marks across relevant banking and financial services, reassess whether ASPIRE is relatively weak or strong, and then reexamine whether ASPIRE and ASPIRE BANK convey sufficiently distinct commercial impressions. Two outcomes are plausible:

- If the Board finds ASPIRE to be weak (many third‑party uses of Aspire in banking and closely related financial services), it will likely construe CC Serve’s protection narrowly. Under that narrower scope, the addition of the descriptive term “BANK” could be enough—especially in combination with any stylization—to reduce the likelihood of confusion. The first factor would weigh less strongly for CC Serve because consumers are accustomed to different Aspire marks within the same field.

- If the Board finds ASPIRE to be relatively strong (few or no relevant third‑party Aspire marks in banking/finance), CC Serve’s ASPIRE will have broader protection. In that event, the shared dominant term “ASPIRE” will likely carry more weight, and ASPIRE BANK may be viewed as producing a similar commercial impression likely to cause source confusion, particularly given the parties’ highly similar services.

Either way, the Board’s next round of analysis must be consistent: the scope used to define similar services (the second factor) should be mirrored in the scope of third‑party marks considered for the sixth factor, and the revised strength assessment must inform the first‑factor comparison.

Conclusion

The Apex decision reinforces two enduring lessons. First, the first DuPont factor is a totality inquiry about how consumers perceive marks in context; courts should not permit narrow semantic parsing or overreliance on disclaimers to defeat that inquiry. Second, a mark’s strength—shaped by third‑party use in the relevant field—modulates how much difference between marks is required to avoid confusion. Practitioners evaluating or litigating mark similarity should therefore (1) define carefully the relevant marketplace and goods/services, (2) assemble comprehensive third‑party evidence across that defined scope, and (3) conduct the first‑factor comparison with the assessed strength in mind. Apex is a remedial lesson: an erroneous narrowing of the field of comparison can vitiate the commercial‑impression analysis and require reconsideration of whether the marks, in their entireties, are likely to confuse.

# Appellate Review, Remand, and Strategic Takeaways from Apex Bank v. CC Serve

This chapter synthesizes the legal and practical lessons of Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., a Federal Circuit decision that illustrates how appellate review disciplines Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) DuPont analyses and how errors in one factor ripple across others. Apex is a useful vehicle for teaching three closely related points: (1) the governing standards of appellate review and the common remedies (affirm-in-part, vacate-in-part, remand); (2) the doctrinal need for coherence across DuPont factors—especially when the Board has already made a finding about the scope of similarity between services; and (3) practical record-building and litigation strategies that parties should use in Section 2(d) disputes involving crowded, aspirational terms in the financial-services sector.

## Standards of review and remedies: affirm-in-part, vacate-in-part, and remand; identifying and preserving legal errors

Two distinct standards of review govern appeals from TTAB decisions. The Federal Circuit reviews the Board’s legal conclusions de novo and its factual findings for substantial evidence. Apex reiterates that split: legal questions—e.g., whether the Board applied the correct legal standard in construing which third-party uses are relevant—are reviewed without deference; factual determinations—e.g., how many third-party marks actually exist and how they have been used—survive unless no reasonable factfinder could accept the Board’s conclusions.

Those standards determine available remedies. Appellate courts often affirm some determinations while vacating others and remanding for further proceedings. Apex exemplifies that approach: the court affirmed the Board’s factual finding that the parties’ services were highly similar (second DuPont factor) because substantial evidence supported it, but vacated the Board’s statutory analysis under the sixth factor because the Board applied an inconsistent and impermissibly narrow scope of “similarity” when evaluating third-party marks. The court also vacated the first-factor analysis (similarity of the marks in their entireties) because a re-evaluation of mark strength under factor six could materially alter commercial impression.

For practitioners, two lessons follow. First, preserve legal arguments below. If the Board misapplies a legal standard—by, for example, changing the relevant scope of comparison between factors—object on the ground that the Board’s approach is internally inconsistent and identify the specific legal rule (e.g., that similarity for factor six need not be narrower than similarity already found in factor two). Raising and developing that argument in the Board record maximizes the chances of de novo appellate review and a vacatur/remand. Second, frame the remedy you seek: if an erroneous legal standard infected only some factors, ask the court to vacate those parts and remand rather than seeking wholesale reversal. Apex illustrates why partial vacatur/remand is common: the court upheld the Board’s factfinding where supported by evidence but required reconsideration where legal error tainted the analysis.

## Doctrinal coherence and factor interdependence: maintaining consistent scopes of 'similarity' across the second and sixth factors and their downstream effect on the first factor

The DuPont framework asks tribunals to evaluate multiple, interrelated factors. Apex emphasizes that these factors are not hermetically sealed; findings in one factor can—and frequently should—influence the scope and analysis of others. Two specific points from the opinion are important doctrinally.

First, the Board’s finding under the second DuPont factor—that credit card services and broader banking/finance services are “highly similar” or partially legally identical—fixed the operative scope of relatedness between the parties’ services. Once the Board established that scope, it was arbitrary to constrict the class of third-party marks considered under the sixth factor to only those used with the narrow subcategory of “credit card services.” The Federal Circuit’s remand rests on a basic principle: when the Board has already defined the relevant universe of services as broad (banking + credit-related services), the sixth-factor inquiry into third-party uses must consider marks used across that same universe of services, not a narrower subset. This aligns with earlier precedent holding that sixth-factor comparisons need only show similarity, not identity (Olde Tyme Foods; Juice Generation).

Second, because the first DuPont factor (similarity of the marks in sight, sound, and commercial impression) depends in significant part on the commercial context in which the marks operate, any recalibration of mark strength under factor six may change the ultimate commercial impression inquiry. Apex therefore remanded the first factor for reconsideration once the Board reexamines third-party evidence more broadly. Practically, when a mark is shown to be conceptually or commercially weak (because similar marks are common across the relevant field), the scope of protection narrows and the degree of similarity required to find likelihood of confusion increases. Conversely, a strong mark receives broader protection. DuPont analyses must therefore be coherent: determinations about the market and relatedness should not be narrowed in one factor only to be broadened in another.

## Record-building strategies: collecting and authenticating third-party use evidence; choosing persuasive comparators in adjacent financial services; leveraging third-party registrations

Apex turned on third-party use and registration evidence. Building an effective record under the sixth DuPont factor requires both quantity and quality of evidence. Below are practical steps and evidentiary best practices.

- Define the relevant market early and consistently. If you claim the relevant consumers are “banking and financial-services consumers,” collect third-party evidence across that category instead of limiting discovery or exhibits to a single subcategory such as “credit cards.” Apex shows the danger of self-limiting your universe.

- Collect contemporaneous proof of use. Use screenshots, marketing materials, customer-facing documents (statements, welcome kits), bank disclosures, press releases announcing product launches, and advertising invoices. Where possible, capture dates and URLs, and archive pages (Wayback Machine, PDF captures) to show continuous or historical use and timing relative to the parties’ priority dates.

- Authenticate and explain relevance. Affidavits or declarations from business records custodians or attorneys-of-record explaining how exhibits were collected and what they show will reduce the Board’s (and later the court’s) reason to discount the material. Show how each third-party mark is used in connection with the relevant services (e.g., “Aspire” used as the brand for a consumer credit card program versus as a motivational consultancy).

- Demonstrate geographic and channel overlap. Since trade channels and consumer sophistication matter, demonstrate whether third-party uses are national or local, whether they appear in the same distribution channels (online banking portals, mailings, retail bank branches), and whether they target the same consumer segments.

- Use third-party registrations strategically, and distinguish registration evidence from proof of actual use. Registrations are admissible and persuasive for showing that various parties have sought protection for similar marks on similar services; registered examples can be marshalled to show a crowded field. But registrations alone may carry less weight than evidence of marketplace use. Apex shows the Board relied partly on registrations but needed use evidence to assess commercial strength realistically.

- Choose persuasive comparators. In financial services, marks may be used on a spectrum from full-service retail banks to program managers that provide processing but do not issue cards. When selecting comparators, emphasize those that operate in functions directly analogous to the disputed services (card issuance, account servicing, network processing, co-branded bank products). If your opponent relies on a narrow business model (e.g., CC Serve’s role as a program manager working with banks), assemble evidence showing how consumers perceive or encounter the program (branded cards, billing statements, customer service channels) to link the third-party comparators to consumer perceptions.

- Use surveys and consumer-declaration evidence when feasible. Well-designed consumer surveys tailored to the relevant purchasing population can be powerful to show likelihood (or lack) of confusion and to demonstrate consumer sophistication. Declarations from customers, banks, or channel partners detailing how products are marketed and encountered can supplement hard documentary evidence.

## Managing risk for aspirational terms (e.g., 'ASPIRE') in crowded fields: clearance, branding alternatives, and consent agreements

“Aspire” is an archetypal aspirational, laudatory term. Such terms fall toward the descriptive or suggestive end of the distinctiveness spectrum, making them particularly vulnerable in crowded fields. Apex offers practical guidance for different actors.

- For applicants seeking clearance: conduct broad clearance searches that go beyond exact matches and include formative variants (Aspire, Aspiration, AspireBank, ASPIRE BANK) and marks used across related financial services. Because third-party evidence need not show identical goods, search credit card processors, banks, fintechs, loyalty programs, and related fintech service providers. Also search domain names and business registrations.

- Consider narrowing identifications of services. If a proposed mark would be used in a narrow, tiered way (e.g., online-only bank vs. brick-and-mortar retail banking), limiting the spec under the identification of services can avoid overlap with preexisting uses. But beware: narrow identifications may trade off useful statutory breadth for easier registration.

- Use disclaimers and design features. Disclaimers of descriptive elements and distinctive design elements (logos, stylized typefaces, and colors) can help differentiate marks in the eyes of the Board and consumers. However, disclaimers cannot cure initial similarity when commercial impression is dominated by the same dominant word.

- Consider coexistence or consent agreements early. Carefully drafted coexistence agreements that carve out customers, channels, or uses can mitigate enforcement risk and reduce likelihood disputes. The Board gives weight to such agreements in its balancing. Consent agreements should be specific and address the features that create consumer confusion risk.

- If clearance fails, weigh rebranding versus narrowly tailored coexistence. In a crowded field, adopting a more distinctive brand (coining a word, adding an arbitrary element) can be more cost-effective than litigating a weak, aspirational mark.

## How Apex aligns with and extends Palm Bay, Juice Generation, Jack Wolfskin, Coach Services, Bose, and M2 Software

Apex is best understood as applying and reconciling prior Federal Circuit precedent:

- Palm Bay Imps. v. Veuve Clicquot (2005): Palm Bay teaches that third-party use of similar marks is relevant to show a mark's relative weakness. Apex follows that premise but adds a procedural requirement: the Board must consider third-party marks across the same scope of goods/services that it has already found to be similar.

- Juice Generation v. GS Enters. (2015): Juice Generation emphasized that a “considerable number” of third-party uses could demonstrate weakness. Apex applies the logic that the volume of third-party marks matters, but it rejects an artificially constricted universe that excludes otherwise relevant third-party uses.

- Jack Wolfskin v. New Millennium Sports (2015): Jack Wolfskin held that trademark strength can be diminished by pervasive third-party use and that natural consumers can learn to distinguish similar marks when many exist. Apex follows Jack Wolfskin’s concern about commercial strength but requires the Board to assess weakness with a full view of relevant third-party uses.

- Coach Services v. Triumph Learning (2012): Coach Services reiterates that relatedness does not require identity; Apex reaffirms that the second DuPont factor considers whether consumers could reasonably believe the services emanate from the same source even when offered in different form by different parties (program managers vs. banks).

- Bose Corp. v. QSC Audio Prods. (2002) and M2 Software v. M2 Commc’ns (2006): These cases govern standard-of-review principles for TTAB appeals (factual findings tested for substantial evidence; legal conclusions reviewed de novo). Apex applies those rules precisely, affirming fact findings supported by record evidence and vacating legal error.

Apex therefore stands at the intersection of these cases: it applies the third-party weakness analysis from Palm Bay, Juice Generation, and Jack Wolfskin but insists on doctrinal consistency and appropriate scope in evaluating what counts as third-party evidence. The decision does not change the substantive tests; it clarifies the architecture by which DuPont factors must be harmonized.

## Implications for remand: potential recalibration of mark strength and commercial impression; likely outcomes and how they would influence future TTAB practice and applicant strategy

What should parties expect on remand after Apex? The court’s vacatur of the sixth and first factors requires the Board to reconsider mark strength and commercial impression in light of a potentially broader universe of third-party uses. Several outcomes are possible, each carrying predictable consequences.

- Recalibration toward weakness. If the Board, on reconsideration, finds that many Aspire-formative marks are used across banking and finance services (not merely within narrowly defined credit-card products), it may deem CC Serve’s ASPIRE mark commercially weak and entitled to a narrow scope of protection. That could reduce the likelihood-of-confusion risk because the Board would require more significant similarities between the marks for a likelihood finding.

- Recalibration toward strength. Conversely, the Board may find that third-party uses are limited, localized, or superficially similar and thus conclude CC Serve’s mark remains relatively strong. If so, the Board’s first-factor analysis may retain the result that the marks’ similar dominant word creates a likelihood of confusion, particularly when coupled with the affirmed finding of high relatedness under factor two.

- New mixed judgments. The Board might find the mark somewhat weak but still conclude that under the totality of the DuPont factors confusion is likely—especially given the affirmed similarity of services and overlapping trade channels. That result would leave registrants with a reduced monopolistic scope but still unable to register conflicting marks.

The practical effect on TTAB practice and applicant strategy is substantial. First, applicants should anticipate that any factual finding about relatedness will control the scope of later DuPont inquiries; arguing for narrow service identifications is often decisive. Second, registrants should proactively develop documentary evidence showing long-standing, exclusive commercial use, advertising expenditures, and customer recognition to rebut third-party evidence that would diminish strength. Third, both sides should marshal broad-based third-party evidence early rather than relying on narrow illustration; courts will reject self-limiting arguments that produce inconsistent analyses across factors.

Finally, Apex should encourage the TTAB to be explicit about the scope of its similarity findings at each stage of analysis. Clear findings about which consumers, channels, and services are “relevant” will reduce appellate reversals based on inconsistent scope.

Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve reinforces familiar Federal Circuit doctrines—de novo review of legal questions, substantial-evidence review of facts, the relevance of third-party use to mark strength—and adds a practical injunction: DuPont factors must be applied coherently. A finding that services are “highly similar” in the second factor constrains the sixth-factor inquiry into third-party marks and can change first-factor assessments of commercial impression. Practitioners should therefore build records that are broad in scope, authenticate third-party evidence carefully, preserve legal objections about scope, and consider strategic remedies—narrow IDs, disclaimers, consent agreements, or rebranding—when aspirational terms are used in crowded fields. On remand, the Board must reexamine mark strength across the same sweep of services it already found similar—a lesson that will shape litigation and prosecution strategies in the TTAB and beyond.